import java.util.Scanner;

public class DigitCheck {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        try {
            System.out.print("Enter a string: ");
            String inputString = scanner.nextLine().trim();

            if (inputString.matches("\\d+")) {
                System.out.println("The string is valid.");
            } else {
                String numericString = inputString.replaceAll("\\D+", "");
                System.out.println("The input string is invalid.");

                if (numericString.length() == 0) {
                    System.out.println("No digits found in the input string.");
                } else {
                    System.out.println("The following characters can be removed to make the string valid: " + inputString.replaceAll("[^\\d]", ""));
                }
            }
        } catch (Exception ex) {
            System.out.println("An error occurred while processing the input string: " + ex.getMessage());
        } finally {
            scanner.close();
        }
    }
}
```

Here, we use the `Scanner` class to read user input from the command line. We then use the `matches` method of the `String` class to check if the string contains only digit characters. If the string is valid, we simply print a message indicating that it is valid. 

